  MODULE CONTEXT
  This module contains the migration's only security adjacent source edit, and this folder of
  reports is the module's regression net. Because the module's main source was edited, an
  unregistered failure here is a security outcome regression and is blocking rather than a note.
  Described neutrally, and without reproducing any audited text, the edit is this: a class literal
  reference to a vendor internal naming factory was replaced by the identical class name as a
  string, and two values were externalized into Spring configuration unchanged. Of the three
  occurrences in that class, only the class literal field initializer is visible to the compiler;
  the other two are a configuration string literal and a Javadoc block. That distinction matters
  because the static audit gate is textual rather than semantic, so a build that compiles, deploys
  and passes every functional flow can still fail it on the two occurrences no compiler ever sees.
  The remediation changes the context factory field from a class type to a string type with a
  matching accessor and mutator and passes the class name at its single functional use, which the
  naming environment consumes identically; it is therefore a representation change and not a
  behaviour change. Both halves must land together, the source change and the configuration block
  that now hosts the values, or the module deploys without them. The asymmetry that makes the fix
  possible is a property of the platform and not a workaround: compiling against the encapsulated
  class fails, while a runtime lookup of the same class by its name succeeds. That is why the
  register of platform internal access exceptions in production launch configuration, referred to
  by title only, is delivered empty and says so. The zero there is earned: every accessibility
  failure in this migration sat inside a test library and was resolved by upgrading or removing
  that library, every reflective access widening call site in the codebase targets the
  application's own classes, and no platform security manager is installed anywhere.
  This module is the clearest illustration in the whole change set of the transformation rule
  labelled R-T3 here, paraphrased rather than quoted: where a dependency fails under the platform's
  stronger encapsulation the resolution is to upgrade or replace that dependency, and platform
  internal call sites are rewritten to supported APIs rather than accommodated by widening module
  access. The related rule labelled R-2 here is likewise paraphrased and never quoted, so that this
  file carries no literal module access argument token. No module access JVM argument that opens or
  exports an otherwise encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module is relied on by this evidence,
  and none is present in the production launch configuration. Such an argument was never a viable
  shortcut in any case, quite apart from being forbidden: because the audit gate is textual, it
  would have left every occurrence in place and failed the gate on an otherwise working build, so
  the rule and the gate independently foreclose the same shortcut.
  This report is NOT offered as proof that the directory context edit preserves behaviour, and its
  passing outcome must not be read as that proof. None of the module's seven unit test classes
  exercises the remediated class, this one included, which was verified across the module's whole
  test tree rather than assumed. What this report does establish is narrower and still worth
